From: Rob Lymm <rob@mediaeden.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: add msm8974 support

msm8974 has 3 CSIPHY, 4 CSID, 1 ISPIF and 2 VFE. The blocks are the same
generation as msm8916 -- 2-phase CSIPHY, CSID 4.1, VFE 4.1 -- but there are
more of them, and the ISPIF is v3.0, which behaves like msm8996's.

That split is why this adds CAMSS_8x74 rather than reusing CAMSS_8x16. Of the
places camss branches on SoC version, msm8974 goes with 8x16 for the CSID
source-pad format and the VFE source-pad format, and with 8x96 for the ISPIF
register layout, line count and interrupt handler. Neither existing value is
correct on its own: msm8974 enumerates four ISPIF lines, where 8x16 would give
two.

msm8974's camera subsystem has no IOMMU, so camss cannot depend on IOMMU_DMA
and cannot hand the VFE a scatter-gather list. Choose the buffer allocator
from whether the device is actually behind an IOMMU, rather than by SoC:
contiguous memory from CMA where there is none, scatter-gather where an
IOMMU can make a fragmented buffer look contiguous.

The VFE clock_rate table stops at 400 MHz rather than following
ftbl_camss_vfe_vfe0_1_clk to 465 MHz. That last entry is
F(465000000, P_MMPLL3, ...), but vfe0_clk_src's parent map in mmcc-msm8974 is
mmcc_xo_mmpll0_mmpll1_gpll0_map, which has no MMPLL3, so the rate is
unreachable and clk_round_rate returns -ENOENT. It matters because with no
sensor attached camss asks for the highest rate in the list.

Every other clock the tables name is already in mmcc-msm8974. msm8974 has no
plain CAMSS_AHB_CLK, so unlike msm8916 there is no "ahb" entry; "top_ahb" is
CAMSS_TOP_AHB_CLK. Both VFEs share one CAMSS_VFE_GDSC, so there is no per-VFE
has_pd and the power domain belongs on the camss node, as on msm8916.

Tested on an Xperia Z2 (msm8974pro): the driver probes clean, media-ctl shows
the full CSIPHY -> CSID -> ISPIF -> VFE graph with three CSIPHYs, four CSIDs,
four ISPIF lines and two VFEs of three RDI lines each, and a capture from
CSID0's test pattern generator produces correct frames -- 1920x1080 SRGGB10
packed, 2592000 bytes each, carrying the expected ramp.

